# reconcile — success
#
# Making peace with somebody who counts you an enemy: forced past the dice.
#
# --npc-hostile is what makes this reachable: reconcile applies only to an enemy (or an annoying
# acquaintance), and unlike appease it needs somebody who can SPEAK — so --spawn-beast does not help,
# a wolf cannot be reconciled with. Earning a human enemy in script means committing a crime, being
# caught, losing the confrontation and walking out of the fight, which would make this a test of all
# of that instead.
#
# --auto-dialogue settles the reconcile tree this verb opens; the trees are covered separately by
# cli/_systems/dialogue_*.cli.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --npc-hostile --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Hall" --period dawn --observe-only "brewer"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

strategy succeed
goal reconcile
advance 12 180
regions

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect SUCCESS
# The OUTCOME, not just the banner. "SUCCESS" is the dice result — it is printed from
# result.Succeeded and says nothing about what the verb did, so a verb whose SuccessReports
# stopped producing anything would still pass every other assertion here. This is the chip
# the outcome block carries, and it is the only line that proves the verb had an effect.
expect Conversation:

# What makes this a reconcile test rather than an any-verb test.
expect-verb reconcile
quit
